          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which biochemical deficiency primarily explains why anaerobic bacteria cannot su</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which biochemical deficiency primarily explains why anaerobic bacteria cannot survive in well-oxygenated tissues?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lack of peptidoglycan synthesis enzymes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Peptidoglycan synthesis is essential for cell-wall formation in most bacteria but is unrelated to oxygen tolerance.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Absence of catalase and/or superoxide dismutase</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Without catalase and superoxide dismutase, anaerobes cannot detoxify reactive oxygen species (superoxide, hydrogen peroxide), making aerobic environments lethal.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Deficiency of cytochrome oxidase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cytochrome oxidase is part of the aerobic electron transport chain; its absence limits aerobic energy production but does not directly cause oxygen toxicity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inability to produce lipopolysaccharide</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lipopolysaccharide is a Gram-negative outer-membrane component (endotoxin) and is not involved in oxygen detoxification.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key is oxygen detoxification. Anaerobes lack catalase and superoxide dismutase, so reactive oxygen species (superoxide radicals, H₂O₂) accumulate and are lethal. This is why they colonise only low-redox, devitalised sites."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Anaerobes lack catalase and/or superoxide dismutase, so toxic oxygen intermediates accumulate and kill them — this is why they flourish only in devitalised, ischaemic tissue where the redox potential drops below –300 mV."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
