          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with a sharply demarcated, raised, bright-red plaque on the l</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with a sharply demarcated, raised, bright-red plaque on the leg involving superficial lymphatics. Which organism is most likely responsible?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Staphylococcus aureus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>S. aureus typically causes cellulitis (deeper, poorly demarcated) or follicular infections (furuncles, carbuncles), not the sharply demarcated raised lesion of erysipelas.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Streptococcus)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Erysipelas is caused by S. pyogenes and characteristically presents as a sharply demarcated, raised, bright-red plaque involving the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bacteroides fragilis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bacteroides fragilis is an obligate anaerobe causing intra-abdominal infections and deep abscesses, not superficial skin plaques.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Propionibacterium acnes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>P. acnes is associated with acne vulgaris and post-implant infections, not acute erythematous skin plaques.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "This is the classic description of erysipelas — a sharply demarcated, raised, bright-red plaque with superficial lymphatic involvement, caused by Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Strep). The sharp borders and raised edge distinguish it from cellulitis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The sharply demarcated, raised border with superficial lymphatic involvement is the hallmark of erysipelas, caused by Streptococcus pyogenes. In contrast, cellulitis (S. aureus or S. pyogenes) has poorly defined, non-raised margins and extends into deeper subcutaneous tissue."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
