          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Why does haematogenous osteomyelitis in children most commonly affect the metaph</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why does haematogenous osteomyelitis in children most commonly affect the metaphysis of long bones?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The metaphysis has the thinnest cortical bone, allowing easy bacterial penetration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cortical thickness is not the determining factor; it is the vascular architecture within the metaphysis that creates a bacterial sanctuary.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The metaphyseal growth plate secretes chemotactic factors that attract circulating bacteria</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The growth plate does not secrete bacterial chemotactic factors; the predilection is explained by haemodynamic factors in the metaphyseal vasculature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sluggish capillary flow in the metaphyseal loop and absence of phagocytic lining cells create a bacterial sanctuary</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In children, arteriolar capillaries form hairpin loops at the metaphysis where blood flow becomes sluggish. The absence of phagocytic lining cells in these sinusoidal vessels allows bacteria to lodge and multiply unopposed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haematogenous spread via Batson's vertebral venous plexus preferentially reaches the metaphysis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Batson's plexus connects pelvic venous drainage to vertebral bodies — it explains vertebral osteomyelitis in adults, not metaphyseal infection in children.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key is the unique vascular anatomy of the metaphysis in children. Capillary loops have sluggish blood flow and lack phagocytic lining cells, creating a bacterial sanctuary. In adults, by contrast, haematogenous osteomyelitis typically targets vertebral bodies via Batson's plexus."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The metaphyseal capillary loops have sluggish flow and lack phagocytic lining cells, creating a sanctuary where bacteria seed and proliferate during bacteraemia — this is why haematogenous osteomyelitis targets the metaphysis in children."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
