          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>While performing a slit-skin smear for leprosy diagnosis, the skin is firmly pin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>While performing a slit-skin smear for leprosy diagnosis, the skin is firmly pinched between the thumb and index finger before making the incision. What is the primary purpose of this step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>To anaesthetise the skin by compressing local nerve endings</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pinching does not provide meaningful anaesthesia; the purpose is related to the quality of the specimen obtained from the slit.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>To bring dermal macrophages closer to the surface for easier collection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While M. leprae resides in macrophages, the pinching step aims to control the type of fluid collected, not to displace cells within the dermis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>To expel blood from the site so that dermal tissue fluid, not blood, is obtained</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Firm pinching renders the site bloodless, ensuring the blade scrapes dermal tissue fluid rich in M. leprae-laden macrophages rather than blood, which would dilute the specimen and reduce diagnostic yield.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>To increase the thickness of the skin fold, allowing a deeper and safer incision</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The incision is a shallow 5 mm slit through the epidermis into the dermis; the pinch is meant to expel blood, not to facilitate deeper cutting.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The pinch is a critical step to make the site bloodless. The 5 mm slit then yields dermal tissue fluid (not blood), which contains the M. leprae-laden macrophages needed for accurate Ziehl-Neelsen staining and bacteriological index grading."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The skin is pinched firmly to expel blood from the site. This ensures the blade collects dermal tissue fluid — rich in M. leprae-laden macrophages — rather than blood, which would dilute the specimen and reduce the accuracy of bacteriological index grading."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
